          "location": "decision_logic[10].action; forms_records[0]; comparison_tables[4].rows[5]",
          "problem": "The compounded drug preparation label is stated, without qualification, to be affixed 'in addition to the ordinary label elements', including in a condition that covers every Board-licensed pharmacy's compounded preparation. The first sentence of the fourth paragraph of M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 21 ties the section's general labeling provisions to compounding and dispensing on a prescription, so the ordinary dispensing label elements need not attach in every setting the label sentence reaches, e.g. a sterile preparation compounded by a s. 39I institutional pharmacy for administration to its own inpatient (worked_examples[5]).",
          "rationale": "The teaching point that matters, that the compounded-preparation label supplements rather than replaces whatever ordinary label applies, is correct, and the label duty itself is stated accurately with its full reach. The imprecision concerns only whether ordinary label elements always co-exist, which the section expressly teaches elsewhere; it would not lead a candidate to omit a required label on Massachusetts facts. Weighed as Low; a wording such as 'in addition to any ordinary label that applies' would remove it.",
