      "practice_mapping_verdict": "PASS",
      "summary": "Verified every quoted provision against current official text. 247 CMR 9.01(3), (4), (15) and (16) are quoted verbatim (PDF dated 12/6/24), and 247 CMR 2.00 confirms 'licensee', 'simple', 'moderate' and 'complex non-sterile' are undefined, so the section's ordinary-meaning readings and safe-course framing are properly flagged as readings. The statutory USP duty in M.G.L. c. 112, ss. 39G(a)(4), 39H(a)(4), 39I(a)(5) with the (c)(4) no-exemption provisos, and the s. 39D definition of USP (USP and National Formulary, current edition), are accurately distinguished from 9.01(3). M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 21 fourth and fifth paragraphs are accurately taught: the label reaches all preparations compounded by a Board-licensed pharmacy and states no exception; the telephone line reaches pharmacies both engaged in sterile/complex non-sterile compounding and licensed under ss. 39G-39I, staffed during regular hours every day and not less than 56 hours per week, with the narrow s. 39I same-hospital inpatient exception confined to the telephone paragraph. The non-resident treatment (247 CMR 6.02(5)-(6), 6.09, 9.20) is presented as a safe course, not as a text-stated duty. 247 CMR 9.16(7)-(8), 9.19(1)(g)/(j), (23) (area only) and (24) (both) and 247 CMR 14.01 (discretionary 'may waive', MoR signature, 5-year expiry) are correct and the waiver is stated with the Board's discretionary modality. Board Policy 2023-07 is consistently presented as guidance and its examples and statements are accurately reported. All eight practice questions (MA-Q-0117, 0314, 0316, 0321, 0327, 0399, 0421, 0422) are answerable from the section and consistent with their keys. One Low imprecision about ordinary label elements does not mislead on Massachusetts facts. Publishable as it stands."
